Z||000000||Urchin||10-03-2001||01:49 AM||||The success of a hockey team today seems to be for the most part based on how deep the owner's pockets are.  Anyone who follows hockey is well aware of who these teams are.  Formula 1, I do not follow, but am aware of the fact that it is relatively fixed (I use the term loosely).  You have the top few cars (wealthy teams) battling for the top spot, and the crappy cars battling at the rear (just for the record, I think the F1 field should simply be cut to 10 cars).  As the years go by the NHL is beginning to follow suit.  Unfortunately, the majority of these teams I can't stand (ie. Colorado, Detroit, Dallas, etc.).  But the point of my ramblings is simply to hear some opinions on whether anyone else has noticed this, agrees, etc?  Also unfortunate, is the fact that this may spell more trouble for Canadian franchises later on down the road. <P>On a side note...  tommorrow night, regular season openning!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/final.jpg">  <BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.<p>[This message has been edited by Urchin (edited 10-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Steve Normington||10-03-2001||10:38 AM||||Deep pockets does not insure a good season. Look at the NY Rangers. In nearly every sport or business, more money means more success. If you want to run for Congress, is it better to be qualified and poor or unqualified and rich? If you start a coffee house, is it better to have $1M or $1B startup money?||||reg||1
